# Soft 404s reported by Search Console 2026-08-21. None of these paths have
# ever existed on this site — they're links from an older structure or from
# elsewhere — but the SPA catch-all below answers every unmatched URL with 200
# and a client-rendered Not Found, which Google reads as a soft 404 rather than
# a missing page. Redirecting them turns each one into a clean signal.

# /get-started was a CTA URL on an earlier version of the site and is still
# linked from outside. Booking a call is what it promised, so send it to the
# page that does that rather than let the catch-all answer 200 with a
# client-rendered Not Found.
/get-started    /contact    301

# Literal "$1" — an old redirect rule written Apache-style instead of with
# Netlify's :splat, so the placeholder itself got crawled. The rule is long
# gone but the URL is still in Google's index answering 200.
/$1             /           301

# /case-studies/* was never this site's URL shape; the work lives under /project/*
/case-studies/ring-rival        /project/ring-rival   301
/case-studies/roi-design-builder  /#case-studies      301
/case-studies/*                 /#case-studies        301

# Posts that don't exist here. The trust one has a real counterpart; the other
# two have no close match, so they go to the index rather than to a post that
# only vaguely resembles what the link promised.
/blog/designing-trust-into-ai      /blog/designing-for-trust-when-the-product-is-the-risk   301
/blog/figma-to-production-with-ai  /blog   301
/blog/designing-ai-native-interfaces  /blog   301

# business-management was taken down 2026-08-09 and is back as a full case
# study on the current layout. wholesale-distribution was the older URL for the
# same project, so it points at the page rather than at the index.
/project/wholesale-distribution  /project/business-management   301

# SplitTime hidden 2026-08-22 (splittime.pro is dead). This rule has to live
# HERE, not only in netlify.toml: this file is processed first and its catch-all
# on the last line matches before netlify.toml is ever consulted, so a 301 that
# exists only in the toml never fires for a path this file does not name.
/project/splittime               /#case-studies   301

# /projects was never a page. It was a client-side <Navigate> to /#case-studies,
# which means React had to boot before anything happened — so the URL answered
# 200 with an empty #root, while still carrying index,follow, a self-referential
# canonical, its own OG card and sitemap priority 0.9. Every non-JS crawler and
# answer engine saw a blank document at the site's most-linked URL after the
# homepage, and the five rules above were funnelling retired-URL equity into it.
#
# It is a redirect, so it belongs here rather than in the router. Forced (301!)
# because rules in this file do not shadow real files by default, and this must
# fire even if a stale projects/index.html ever survives into a deploy.
#
# The work itself is not moving: /#case-studies is the homepage section that has
# always been the real index, and /project/* detail pages are untouched.
/projects                        /#case-studies   301!

# Old flat service URLs. The service pages live under /design-services/*; these
# bare paths are from an earlier structure and are still crawled. Confirmed via
# Search Console 2026-08-29: /web-development was answering 200 with an empty
# #root and no canonical, which Google reads as a soft 404 rather than a move.
/web-development       /design-services/web-development   301
/ux-ui-design          /design-services/ux-ui-design      301
/mobile-app-design     /design-services/mobile-app-design 301
/design-services       /services                          301

# Live products of Hiram's that were never case studies here. Each answered 200
# with a blank document: SimplifiedProjectDetail does redirect them, but
# client-side, so a crawler files the empty 200 as a soft 404 before React runs.
# /project/nudgeme is confirmed in Search Console; the other two are the same
# shape and were verified returning an empty #root on 2026-08-29.
#
# Listed explicitly rather than as /project/* on purpose. A wildcard here would
# be one line instead of three, but it only stays safe as long as non-forced
# rules never shadow the real prerendered case-study files — and if that
# assumption is ever wrong, every case study 301s to the homepage. Not worth
# finding out on a live portfolio to save two lines.
/project/nudgeme       /#case-studies                     301
/project/roi-calculator /#case-studies                    301
/project/quickqr       /#case-studies                     301

# SPA fallback — must stay last. Does not shadow real files, so the
# per-route HTML written by scripts/inject-seo-html.ts is still served.
#
# Points at spa-shell.html, not index.html: index.html now carries the
# homepage's prerendered body, so using it as the catch-all would serve
# homepage content for every unmatched URL — a soft 200 on what should read as
# a 404. spa-shell.html is the same shell with an empty #root, so unmatched
# routes client-render whatever they should (including NotFound).
/*    /spa-shell.html   200
